WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.707
SQL uses three-valued logic. A condition can be TRUE, FALSE, or UNKNOWN.

00:00:04.707 --> 00:00:10.482
Comparing a normal value with NULL gives UNKNOWN, not FALSE.

00:00:10.482 --> 00:00:11.587
And NOT UNKNOWN remains UNKNOWN.

00:00:11.587 --> 00:00:18.742
Because WHERE returns only rows whose condition is TRUE, UNKNOWN behaves like a filtered-out row.

00:00:18.742 --> 00:00:21.622
This explains the NOT IN behavior.

00:00:21.622 --> 00:00:30.712
Connect this idea to a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:30.712 --> 00:00:36.382
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.382 --> 00:00:44.477
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:50.617
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:56.558
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
